Z||000001||Patrick||01-27-2000||03:30 PM||||<BR>I've posted one of the pictures here (shot of the inside) because I wanted to ask something about the removal of the stock one.<P> <IMG SRC="http://home.ptd.net/~clintn/ebay/holleyb.jpg"> <P>When the top two screws are removed, how are the two tabs held in the slots on the body of the car? Is there a certain angle the unit should be held at as it's pulled out? I don't want to force mine, but it seems really stuck.||||reg||5
Z||000002||Clint||01-27-2000||03:58 PM||||no real special angle that i can remember.  Just tip it out a little and pull.  i would say 45 degrees at the most.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||01-27-2000||10:01 PM||||<BR>Thanks Clint, that certainly sounds simple enough.||||reg||14
Z||000004||cobra427||01-27-2000||11:35 PM||||Pat, If I remember correctly, it is advised to use longer screws when installing the scoop. I did on mine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||01-28-2000||12:22 AM||||<BR>Actually, the reason I want to remove the factory air intake is to see what would be involved in making my own scoop. But because I've never had the factory intake off before, I didn't know how aggressive to get when pulling those tabs out of the holes. Like I said earlier, they really seem to be stuck in there.||||reg||14
Z||000006||Rodrv6||01-28-2000||12:43 AM||||On my intake, the tabs on the bottom have little serrated lock tabs built into them.  I had to reach up under the rocker panel and squeeze the lock tabs in order to pull the intake grill out of the fender.||||reg||4
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||01-28-2000||12:48 AM||||I found out the hard way, so let me tell you so that you know not to do it. Don't tighten TOO much or you could crack the fiberglass! <P>I didn't know my own strength...||||reg||4
Z||000008||Patrick||01-28-2000||12:51 AM||||<BR>Ahhhh..... lock tabs! That makes sense. No wonder the damn intake didn't want to come off. Thanks Rod.||||reg||4
